<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; color: #333;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: -10px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage Time</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist in negotiations between student groups. Concept: Interpret graphical data for classroom use.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist groups in negotiating study-related tasks by rephrasing ideas simply (e.g., asks, "Can we all agree on study times?" while showing two options: "morning or afternoon" on the board). Can interpret basic charts or schedules related to study habits by highlighting key times (e.g., explaining a sample study schedule, pointing to "3-4 p.m." for after-school study time). Can clarify group tasks by explaining simple data from a graph, like a bar chart on "study hours per week" (e.g., "More time in English can help.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various simple icons or images related to study habits (e.g., a clock, a calendar, a book, a group of students discussing). Students, in pairs, quickly explain what each icon means for their study routine or the "Time Management Poster" project. The teacher encourages them to use vocabulary like 'time', 'organize', 'goals', 'routines'.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a simple bar chart showing "Average Weekly Study Hours per Subject" for an imaginary class. The teacher models how to interpret the data and suggest an action, using mediating language: "This chart shows that students spend more time on Math (3 hours) than English (1 hour). Perhaps we could suggest more focused study on English to achieve better grades. What do you think? Can we agree that more effort in English might improve our results?" The teacher also models rephrasing: "If Student A says, 'I can't work after 5 PM,' and Student B says, 'I can't work before 7 PM,' a mediator might say, 'So, Student A prefers early evening, and Student B prefers late evening. Can we try to find a time that works for both?'" The teacher emphasizes clear pronunciation of /st/ in "study" and /s/ in "success."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a friend doesn't understand a group decision, what should you do to mediate?" (Expected answer: "Rephrase it simply," "Explain it in different words.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I showed the study hours chart, what was my job?" (Expected answer: "Interpret the data," "Explain the key information.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (or written transcript) between three students, Ana, Carlos, and Sofia, discussing their progress on the "Time Management Poster" project. They mention differing ideas for the poster's design, preferred study times, and how they have or haven't managed their time. The dialogue includes phrases like "I used to procrastinate," "I have developed better habits," "What have you done to improve?", and refers to a simple "Weekly Study Schedule" chart (projected on the board) that shows their individual ideal study blocks.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interpret specific data from a schedule and understand the nuances of negotiating group tasks. They will recognize the present perfect for achievements and past simple for reflecting on past habits.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main challenge the students are facing with their 'Time Management Poster' project?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match each student (Ana, Carlos, Sofia) to their preferred study block according to the 'Weekly Study Schedule' chart.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Ana has successfully developed better study habits." "Carlos used to organize his assignments well."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing rephrasing, explaining data, and assisting negotiation. Use of present perfect for achievements ("I have developed," "We have organized"), past simple for reflection ("I used to procrastinate," "They worked on"), WH-questions ("What have you achieved?", "When could we meet?"). Vocabulary: manage, procrastinate, improve, develop, reflect, suggest, set (goals), organize, discuss, productivity, routines, strategies.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Rephrasing Complex Ideas (Controlled Practice):</b> Teacher provides a list of complex statements related to study habits or group work (e.g., "The lack of synchronous interaction among team members has hindered the efficacious implementation of our collective assignment.") and asks students to rephrase them using simpler language, as a mediator would. Example: "We haven't worked together enough, so our assignment is suffering." Students practice the pronunciation of '-ed' endings like in "improved" /d/ and "accomplished" /t/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Interpreting Data for Negotiation (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher projects a simple bar chart showing "Group A's Preferred Study Hours" vs. "Group B's Preferred Study Hours" (e.g., morning vs. afternoon). Students work in pairs. One student acts as a mediator, interpreting the chart and formulating a question to suggest a compromise, using phrases like "It seems that...", "Can we agree on...", "What if we...".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Mediation Dialogue Practice (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in groups of three. Two students present a small disagreement or confusion about a study task (e.g., "I think we should include specific app names on the poster," "I think the poster should be more general"). The third student acts as a mediator, listening to both sides, rephrasing their points, clarifying misunderstandings using WH-questions, and suggesting a resolution. They rotate roles. Encourage the use of "I have developed" (stressing 'developed') or "I used to procrastinate" in their disagreements or proposed solutions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation and Mediated Discussion of the "Time Management Poster".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students, in their pre-assigned groups, present their "Time Management Poster" to the class. Each group must designate one "mediator" for the presentation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediator's role is crucial:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They explain the poster's content, particularly any visual data like a sample study schedule or a chart on "study hours per week" by highlighting key times or strategies.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They rephrase and clarify any complex ideas or different viewpoints that arose within their group during the poster's creation (e.g., "Initially, Maria wanted to focus on digital tools, while Juan preferred traditional methods. We have agreed to include both.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When other groups or the teacher ask questions, the mediator paraphrases the question for their group members, and then rephrases the group's answer for clarity to the audience, assisting in the negotiation of ideas and feedback.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use the present perfect to reflect on achievements ("We have developed a consistent routine for our group work") and past simple to reflect on past habits ("I used to procrastinate on starting assignments, but now I prioritize").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should explicitly incorporate examples of time management techniques adapted to their group's context, as per the sociolinguistic competence (e.g., "We should use the Pomodoro technique, where we study for 25 minutes, then take a 5-minute break. This helps us stay focused without feeling overwhelmed.").</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students are encouraged to ask questions, promoting interaction and giving the mediators more opportunities to practice their skill.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group's designated mediator will lead a brief Q&A session after their "Time Management Poster" presentation, demonstrating their ability to interpret data, rephrase ideas, and assist in negotiations with the teacher or other groups.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Mediator Role):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Simplicity:</b> Can rephrase complex ideas or questions simply and clearly for group members and audience (Learning Outcome).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Data Interpretation:</b> Accurately interprets graphical data or schedules from the poster, highlighting key information (Learning Outcome).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Negotiation Assistance:</b> Effectively assists in discussions or minor negotiations within the group or with the audience (Specific Objective).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar & Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate and accurate use of present perfect (e.g., "I have improved my grades"), past simple (e.g., "I used to procrastinate"), and target vocabulary (study, habits, manage, organize).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, with attention to '-ed' endings and verb stress in 'have developed'.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete individually:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one specific strategy you used today to help your group clarify an idea or agree on a point?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Think about your own study habits. What is one positive change you have developed during this project?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection to future learning</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of mediating—explaining ideas, interpreting data, or helping with agreements—did I find most challenging today, and why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the mediation skills I practiced today, like rephrasing or clarifying, be useful in my academic life beyond English class?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students effectively embody the mediator role during the poster presentations and Q&A? Were they able to rephrase ideas simply and interpret graphical data accurately?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the B1.1 CEFR linguistic content (present perfect, past simple, target vocabulary) integrated naturally into their mediation efforts? Were pronunciation points like '-ed' endings and verb stress noticeable?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks to further develop their negotiation and clarification skills, especially with more complex information or group dynamics?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today, you successfully mediated discussions and interpreted data for your 'Time Management Posters.' These skills are crucial for understanding and communicating across cultures. In our next theme, 'Global Citizenship,' we will apply these mediation abilities to understand and explain diverse perspectives on global issues, helping us to bridge cultural gaps and find common ground."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>